.TH FTPDFIX 1M "7 November 2026" "ftpdfix 2.1-1"
.SH NAME
in.ftpd, ftpdfix \- the FTP server, with downloads that arrive whole
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/sbin/in.ftpd
.SH DESCRIPTION
The ftpdfix package replaces
.BR /usr/sbin/in.ftpd ,
the system's FTP server, which
.BR inetd (1M)
starts for each connection, with the stock program changed in one
place: how it closes the data connection of a download
.RB ( RETR ,
.BR LIST ,
.BR NLST ).
Everything else, commands, options and messages, is as
.BR ftpd (1M)
describes.
.PP
The stock server closed the data connection as soon as it had written
the file, and the system's TCP then threw away whatever the client had
not yet acknowledged: the end of a download could be missing, while the
server still answered
.BR "226 Transfer complete" .
In passive mode this happened often.
In active mode the stock server lingered in the close instead, for up to
60 seconds, a path of the system's TCP that is not safe when the client
sends after the close.
.PP
This server shuts down its own side of the data connection (the client
then sees the end of the file after the last byte), waits until the
client has read everything and closed its side, and only then closes the
connection.
The wait is bounded: a client that never closes its side is let go after
at most 60 seconds, and the connection is then closed as before.
Nothing lingers in the close, in either mode.
.PP
A client that reads a download to its end and then closes the data
connection, as every FTP client does, sees no difference but whole
files.
.SH FILES
.TP 26
.B /opt/amix/doc/ftpdfix/README.md
the bytes changed and why
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ftpd (1M),
.BR inetd (1M),
.BR ftp (1)
